    "intro": "Everything you have learned so far about investigating an intrusion assumes something you may not have noticed you were assuming: that the server exists, that you can walk to it, image its disk, and pull its logs from an agent you control. The moment your organization moves a workload to AWS, Azure, or Google Cloud, that assumption breaks. The hypervisor is not yours. The physical disk is not yours. In many cases the operating system underneath the service is not even something you can see. And yet the incidents keep happening — credentials get stolen, buckets get exposed, accounts get taken over — and someone still has to investigate them. That someone is you, and the skills transfer less cleanly than most analysts expect.\n\nThis lesson exists because cloud security is not 'the same SOC work but with different log names.' It is a genuinely different threat model built on a genuinely different trust boundary, and analysts who treat it as a reskin of on-prem investigation miss the incidents that matter most. In the cloud, identity does the job that network segmentation and physical access used to do, which means a leaked access key can be more dangerous than a remote code execution exploit. Misconfiguration — not malware — is the single most common cause of cloud breaches, because a public S3 bucket or an overly permissive IAM policy requires no exploit at all, just a mistake and someone who finds it. And the audit trail itself — CloudTrail, Activity Log — becomes both your best source of truth and, if an attacker is sophisticated enough to disable it, a critical alarm bell in its own right.\n\nBy the end of this lesson you will understand the shared responsibility model well enough to know, for any given cloud incident, whether the failure was the provider's or your organization's. You will understand why identity is the new perimeter and how attackers escalate privilege through IAM the way they used to escalate through Windows tokens. You will know what CloudTrail and Azure Activity Log actually record, and why a StopLogging event deserves the same urgency as a ransomware alert. You will recognize the handful of misconfigurations responsible for most real-world cloud breaches. And you will walk through the cloud attack patterns you are most likely to see in a SOC queue — impossible travel, cryptomining from stolen credentials, S3 exfiltration, SSRF against instance metadata, and OAuth consent abuse — along with the specific fields that confirm or dismiss each one.",
