        "heading": "Identity Is the New Perimeter",
        "content": "In a traditional on-prem network, the perimeter was physical and topological: a firewall, a DMZ, a VPN concentrator, a network segment an attacker had to breach before they could touch anything valuable. In the cloud, that topology mostly does not exist in the same way — a resource can be reachable from the entire internet by design, protected not by where it sits on a network diagram but by who is allowed to authenticate to it. This is the single biggest mental shift a SOC analyst has to make: in the cloud, **identity is the perimeter**.\n\nA valid set of credentials with the right permissions can create, modify, or destroy resources from anywhere in the world, instantly, with no need to breach a network boundary at all. This is why a leaked access key is frequently more dangerous than a remote code execution exploit — the exploit gets you a foothold on one machine; the key can get you the keys to the entire environment.\n\n### IAM users, roles, and policies\n\nCloud identity is built from a few core primitives that every analyst needs fluency in:\n\n- **User** — a persistent identity, typically tied to a human or a service, often with long-lived credentials (an access key ID and secret).\n- **Role** — a temporary identity that something (a user, an application, another service) can *assume* to gain a set of permissions for a limited time, without ever holding a long-lived credential of its own.\n- **Policy** — the actual permission document, a JSON structure of allowed and denied actions on specific resources, attached to a user, group, or role.\n\nThe security industry's strong preference, and the direction every major provider is pushing customers, is toward roles and temporary credentials over long-lived user access keys, precisely because a role's credentials expire in minutes or hours while a leaked access key can be valid indefinitely until someone notices and rotates it.\n\n### Access key leakage is a top-tier cloud incident, not a routine one\n\nAccess keys end up in public GitHub repositories, in client-side JavaScript, in CI/CD logs, in container images, and in employee laptops that get compromised through ordinary phishing. Once an attacker has a valid key, they typically do not need to exploit anything further — they authenticate directly to the cloud API with the same rights the key's owner had.\n\nAutomated scanners now trawl public code repositories specifically hunting for exposed AWS and Azure keys, and the time between a key being pushed to a public repo and an attacker using it is frequently measured in minutes. This is why key rotation policies, secret-scanning on commits, and short-lived credentials are not compliance checkbox items — they are the direct answer to the highest-frequency real-world cloud compromise vector.\n\n**Privilege escalation through IAM is the cloud's version of Windows token abuse.** An attacker who lands with a low-privilege identity will often look for IAM misconfigurations that let them grant themselves more. Common escalation paths include:\n\n- A policy that allows a user to attach a new, more permissive policy to their own identity.\n- A role with an overly broad 'PassRole' permission that lets them run code as a more privileged role.\n- A permission to create a new access key for a higher-privileged user.\n\nThese escalation paths do not require any traditional exploit; they require only reading the permission structure carefully, which is exactly what tools like the open-source privilege-escalation scanners built for AWS IAM are designed to automate for both defenders and attackers. As an analyst, this means that a CloudTrail event showing an identity attaching a new policy to itself, or creating an access key for another user, deserves the same scrutiny as a Windows event showing token impersonation — it is very often privilege escalation in progress.\n\n### What this means for triage\n\nWhen you investigate any cloud alert, the first question is not 'what host was this on' — there may not be a host at all — but 'what identity performed this action, and is that identity's behavior consistent with its normal pattern?' A service role that has only ever called three specific API actions for six months, suddenly calling 'CreateAccessKey' or 'AttachUserPolicy', is a stronger signal than almost any network-level indicator you will find in the cloud. Identity behavior baselining is, in the cloud, the rough equivalent of host-based behavior baselining on-prem — and it is where the majority of your investigative attention belongs.",
        "codeExample": "// IAM primitives an analyst must recognize immediately\n//\n// USER   -> persistent identity, often long-lived access key + secret\n//           risk: key leakage (GitHub, CI logs, client-side JS) = instant compromise\n// ROLE   -> temporary identity, assumed for a limited session\n//           preferred over long-lived keys; credentials expire in minutes/hours\n// POLICY -> JSON doc of Allow/Deny actions on resources, attached to user/role\n//\n// PRIVILEGE ESCALATION SIGNS in CloudTrail — treat these like Windows token abuse:\n//   iam:AttachUserPolicy / iam:PutUserPolicy   -> identity granting itself more rights\n//   iam:CreateAccessKey (on ANOTHER user)      -> minting creds for a higher-priv identity\n//   sts:AssumeRole (unusual role, new source)  -> pivoting to broader permissions\n//   iam:PassRole + lambda:CreateFunction        -> running code as a more privileged role\n//\n// Example CloudTrail event fragment to recognize:\n// {\n//   \"eventName\": \"AttachUserPolicy\",\n//   \"userIdentity\": { \"type\": \"IAMUser\", \"userName\": \"svc-report-gen\" },\n//   \"requestParameters\": {\n//     \"userName\": \"svc-report-gen\",\n//     \"policyArn\": \"arn:aws:iam::aws:policy/AdministratorAccess\"\n//   },\n//   \"sourceIPAddress\": \"203.0.113.44\"\n// }\n// -> A low-privilege service account attaching AdministratorAccess TO ITSELF\n//    from an unfamiliar IP is privilege escalation, not routine admin work."
