        "heading": "Anatomy of a Cloud Credential Compromise",
        "content": "Everything covered in this section — leaked keys, IAM privilege escalation, CloudTrail — plays out in a recognizable order once an attacker actually has a foothold, and seeing that order as a single chain makes it far easier to recognize mid-investigation than reading each stage in isolation. A stolen key does not usually sit unused; within minutes it is authenticating directly to the cloud API, orienting itself with read-only reconnaissance calls, then reaching for broader permissions, then — if the attacker is disciplined — blinding the audit trail before doing anything destructive. Recognizing which link in this chain a given CloudTrail event belongs to tells you both what already happened and what to expect next.",
        "codeExample": "flowchart LR\n  A[\"Leaked access key\\nfound in a public repo\"] --> B[\"Authenticate directly\\nto the cloud API\"]\n  B --> C[\"Recon calls\\nListBuckets GetCallerIdentity\"]\n  C --> D[\"Privilege escalation\\nAttachUserPolicy to self\"]\n  D --> E[\"Disable logging\\nStopLogging\"]\n  E --> F[\"Impact\\nexfiltration or cryptomining\"]"
